# The Abbot of St. Markovia, in his chapel (docs/voice/abbot.md). A celestial sent long ago to keep the valley's faith,
# bent by four centuries of watching it suffer: he means to cure the devil with a bride. He vouches for the party at
# Krezk's gate, asks for a wedding dress (offering to heal Ilya), and hands over to the unveiling when it arrives.
# The Abbey is one of the scariest places in the game: his humour is the dreadful calm of the very sure.

~ start
if flag.abbot_fate == "wedding"
-> after_wedding
elif flag.abbot_fate == "broken"
-> after_broken
endif
if not flag.abbot_met
-> first
endif
if flag.wards_cells_freed and not flag.abbot_cells_noted
-> cells
endif
Abbot: Children. You came back. Sit, if you like. The pews are hard, but they are honest about it.
-> menu

~ first
set abbot_met
quest the_abbots_bride met
Narrator: A young monk in a plain brown habit is lighting the candles along the altar rail. He doesn't use a taper. He touches each wick with a fingertip, and each one lights.
Narrator: He turns. Golden hair, a face like a saint carved by someone who had only heard saints described. For a moment the light in the chapel seems to arrange itself around him, and then it remembers itself.
Abbot: Welcome. I hoped you would come today. I am the Abbot of this house. You are cold, and tired, and a little afraid of me. All three are sensible. Two of them I can help with.
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: The light leaned toward him. I have never seen light do that. Not for anyone.
if name:liriel_dawnsong
Narrator: Liriel takes one step toward him and goes down on both knees on the cold flags, her hands over her mouth. When she looks up her face is wet and shining.
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: You're one of His. An angel of the Morninglord, here, in the dark. Oh, I knew it. I knew He hadn't forgotten this place.
Abbot [smile]: Get up, little sister. It's been a long time since anyone knelt to me who meant it. Get up before I start to like it.
if not (quest.carry_the_dawn >= the_angel)
quest carry_the_dawn the_angel
endif
endif
if flag.krezk_abbey_directed and not flag.krezk_gate_open
Abbot: Krezk shut its gate on you and pointed up the mountain. They do that. I am where Krezk sends what it would rather not decide about.
endif
if guest:ireena
set abbot_saw_ireena
Narrator: His eyes find Ireena, and every candle in the chapel leans toward her, all at once, as if a door had opened behind her. Then they straighten.
Abbot: Forgive me. For a moment you were someone I have prayed for for a very long time. Welcome, child. Welcome.
interject guest:ireena: I've had warmer welcomes from wolves. They at least looked at the rest of me.
endif
if guest:ezmerelda
interject guest:ezmerelda: That's no monk. Look at his feet. He's standing a finger's breadth off the floor.
Abbot: Hunters always notice the feet. Welcome, Ezmerelda d'Avenir. Your teacher noticed them too, once.
endif
-> menu

~ menu
* Who are you? -> who
* What is this place? -> place
* Who was St. Markovia? -> markovia
* Who are the Belviews to you? -> belviews
* [if not flag.abbot_nature_known] [Religion DC 15] (Something about him is holy, and wrong. What is he?) -> nature_ok | nature_fail
* [Cleric] (Open yourself to the holy in this room. What stands in front of you?) -> divine
* [Paladin] (Let your Divine Sense reach for him.) -> divine
* [if flag.abbot_saw_ireena and guest:ireena] [Insight DC 13] (Watch how he looks at Ireena.) -> ireena_ok | ireena_fail
* [if not flag.abbot_bride_revealed] Clovin says you have work in the wards. What is it? -> bride
* [if flag.abbot_bride_revealed and not flag.abbot_dress_asked and not flag.abbot_dress_refused] What does your bride still need? -> dress_ask
* [if flag.abbot_dress_refused and not flag.abbot_dress_asked] We've reconsidered. We'll find her a dress. -> dress_yes
* [if flag.abbot_dress_asked and not flag.wedding_dress_found and not flag.wards_dress_found] We're still looking for a dress. -> still_looking
* [if not flag.krezk_gate_open and not flag.abbot_vouched] Krezk won't open its gate to us. -> vouch
* [if flag.ilya_heard and not flag.ilya_fate and not flag.abbot_dress_asked] The burgomaster's son is dying. Will you help him? -> ilya
* [if (flag.wedding_dress_found or flag.wards_dress_found) and not flag.wedding_dress_delivered] We've brought a dress for your bride. -> abbey_of_st_markovia/unveiling:deliver
* [name:liriel_dawnsong] [if flag.abbot_bride_revealed and not flag.liriel_asked_abbot and not flag.abbot_fate] Ask him whether he still remembers the sun. -> companions/dawn:sun
* Attack the Abbot. -> attack
* Goodbye. -> bye

~ who
Abbot: The Abbot. There was a name, once. Names are for those who expect to be called, and no one has called me in a very long while.
Abbot: I keep this house. I heal who comes to it. I mend what can be mended, and I am learning, slowly, that almost everything can.
-> menu

~ place
Abbot: A holy woman built it, long ago, for the sick, the mad and the motherless. When she died, her sisters stayed for a time, and then they did not. When I came there was only the snow and the sick.
Abbot: I have kept it open. Anyone who climbs the mountain finds the door unbarred. Few climb it now. Krezk prefers its wall.
-> menu

~ markovia
set markovia_story_heard
Abbot: Markovia. She went up the road to the castle with a handful of the faithful, and she did not come back. Brave. Very brave, and mistaken.
Abbot: One does not cure a fever by killing the patient. One sits with him. One brings him water. One is patient.
if flag.markovia_letter_read
if name:kip_smudgewick
interject name:kip_smudgewick: With respect, sir, that's not what her letter says. I read it. Small print and all.
else
interject tag:blunt: That's not what her letter says.
endif
Abbot: Letters say many things, child. Clovin keeps a great deal of paper. It comforts him.
Narrator: He says it gently, and a little too quickly, and doesn't look at you while he does.
endif
-> menu

~ belviews
Abbot: My family. They came to me dying, all of them, one winter. Now they live, and every year they are a little more than they were. A bird's lightness. A hound's faithfulness. I give them gifts.
Narrator: Across the courtyard, in the wards, something howls with a voice that is nearly a woman's.
Abbot: Not every gift takes. That is the nature of gifts.
-> menu

~ nature_ok
set abbot_nature_known
Narrator: The light around him bends toward him the way water bends toward a drain. He is no man: something sent from far above, of the kind that stands behind the saints in old paintings. Something that has stood here far too long.
-> menu

~ nature_fail
Narrator: He's handsome, and calm, and very still. If there's more to it than that, the chapel isn't telling you.
-> menu

~ divine
set abbot_nature_known
Narrator: You reach for the holy in the room and find it at once, blinding, like the sun through a keyhole. It is in him. It is him. And at the bottom of it, like silt in a well, something has gone sour.
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: He is of the heavens. My Lord's own heavens, I think. And underneath, something has soured, the way milk sours.
if not name:liriel_dawnsong
interject class:cleric: He's celestial. An angel, or the next thing to one. And something in him has turned, the way milk turns.
endif
Abbot: You see clearly, child. Most people only see the face. It is a good face. I was given it to be trusted.
-> menu

~ ireena_ok
Narrator: His eyes keep going back to her. Her hair, her hands, the line of her throat. Not hunger. Worse: the look of a collector at the one piece missing from a set he has spent his whole life completing.
if name:godrick_pendlebrook
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: My lady, stand by me. Closer than that. He'll have to go round me, and I'm a long way round.
else
interject tag:loyal: Stay close to us, Ireena. Closer than that.
endif
-> menu

~ ireena_fail
Narrator: He's kind to her. Kind to everyone. If there's anything else in it, it's buried too deep for you to see.
-> menu

~ bride
set abbot_bride_revealed
Abbot: This valley has a sickness, and the sickness has a name. Loneliness. Its lord has been alone for four hundred years. A lonely man does terrible things. A loved man does not.
Abbot: So I am making him a wife.
Narrator: He says it the way a carpenter says he is making a chair.
Abbot: Vasilka. She waits in the wards, across the courtyard. Go and see her, if you wish. Be gentle. She is not awake.
if name:thistle
interject name:thistle: Made from what? This whole place smells like a dug grave.
else
interject tag:blunt: Made from what?
endif
Abbot: From what Krezk was finished with. Nothing is wasted in this house.
if name:liriel_dawnsong
Narrator: Liriel doesn't say anything at all. She has gone white to the lips, and very still, the way a candle goes still in a room where a door has just closed.
if not (quest.carry_the_dawn >= the_fall)
quest carry_the_dawn the_fall
endif
endif
if guest:ezmerelda
interject guest:ezmerelda: You're stitching a corpse a wedding for Strahd. I've hunted a lot of madmen. You're the calmest.
endif
* He won't love her. -> wont_love
* That's monstrous. -> monstrous
* Go on. -> dress_ask

~ wont_love
Abbot: Not at first. Love is a habit, child. One forms it the way one forms any other. He will have centuries to practise.
-> dress_ask

~ monstrous
Abbot: Everything is, the first time. The first surgeon was a monster. The first priest who said the dead might rise was a madman. I have learned not to mind what things look like at first.
-> dress_ask

~ dress_ask
if flag.abbot_dress_asked
-> menu
endif
Abbot: She lacks one thing. A bride cannot be married in a shroud, and this house has nothing white in it that is fit for her.
if flag.ilya_fate
Abbot: Bring me a wedding dress, and you will have my thanks, and my prayers, which are worth more than you imagine.
else
Abbot: Bring me a wedding dress. In return: the burgomaster's son is dying in Krezk. Dmitri will not ask me. If you bring me what I need, I will go down the mountain and lay my hands on the boy.
set ilya_heard
quest the_burgomasters_son abbot_offer
endif
* We'll find her a dress. -> dress_yes
* No. We won't help you dress a corpse for the devil. -> dress_no

~ dress_yes
set abbot_dress_asked
quest the_abbots_bride asked
Abbot [smile]: Thank you. Look in Krezk. Every village keeps a dress in a chest somewhere, waiting for a girl who did not live to wear it.
approve liriel_dawnsong -3 godrick_pendlebrook -2 kip_smudgewick -1 thistle -1: You agreed to find a wedding dress for the devil's bride
if not flag.krezk_gate_open and not flag.abbot_vouched
-> vouch
endif
-> menu

~ dress_no
set abbot_dress_refused
Abbot: Then not yet. You will change your minds, or you will not. I am patient. Ask the snow how patient I am.
approve liriel_dawnsong +3 godrick_pendlebrook +2 kip_smudgewick +1 thistle +1: You refused to dress a corpse for the devil
-> menu

~ still_looking
Abbot: Look in Krezk. Look in the chests at the feet of the beds of the women who have buried daughters. I am sorry to say it so plainly. Plain things are quickest.
-> menu

~ vouch
set abbot_vouched
Abbot: Dmitri. He fears me. He is right to; fear is a kind of respect. Tell him I vouch for you. He will hate it. He will open the gate.
-> menu

~ ilya
Abbot: I have offered. Dmitri would rather bury the boy than owe me, and Anna would rather owe me than bury him, and so nobody climbs the mountain and the boy burns.
Abbot: But I will make you a bargain, if you will carry it.
if flag.abbot_bride_revealed
-> dress_ask
endif
-> bride

~ cells
set abbot_cells_noted
Abbot [sad]: You opened my wards. They were not ready. They have gone out into the snow, and the snow will keep them, the way it keeps everything up here.
Abbot: That was not mercy, children. That was impatience wearing mercy's coat. I know the difference. I have worn both.
-> menu

~ attack
Narrator: He watches you reach for your weapons, and for the first time something crosses his face that isn't kindness. It looks very like grief.
cutscene abbot_wings
Abbot: In my own chapel. Very well. I will not let you undo my work.
if guest:ezmerelda
interject guest:ezmerelda: Finally. Silver first, questions never.
endif
approve liriel_dawnsong -3 godrick_pendlebrook -1: You drew steel on an angel in his own chapel
combat abbot_wrath

~ bye
if flag.abbot_dress_asked and not flag.wedding_dress_delivered
Abbot: Go with light, children. Come back with something white.
else
Abbot: Go with light. Come back whenever you are hurt. The door is never barred.
endif
-> END

~ after_wedding
Abbot [smile]: She has gone down to him. Clovin drove her. She sat up very straight and asked whether there would be music. There will be. There must be.
if flag.abbot_told_strahd
Abbot: I wrote to him about your red-haired friend, as well. Do not look at me so. He would have found her. I have only saved him the searching.
endif
Abbot: He will love her. Give it time. I have.
-> END

~ after_broken
Narrator: He sits on the altar step with his hands open on his knees, looking at nothing. The candles along the rail are unlit. He doesn't look up, or speak, or seem to know you're here.
if flag.ilya_fate == "healed"
Narrator: The Belviews say he went down the mountain once, at dawn, laid a hand on the burgomaster's boy, and climbed back up without a word. That was the last thing he did.
endif
-> END
